  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1115 AM CDT Sat Jul 18 2026\n\nLate morning water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air \nanalysis reveal longwave ridging is in place atop the majority of \nthe CONUS, leading to a rather nebulous surface pressure field \nacross the central plains. Despite modest large scale subsidence, \nshort range ensembles suggest 850-mb warming will be on the order of \n1 degree C, which will translate to afternoon temperatures nudging \nupward with highs in the low to mid 90s. Similar to the previous \nseveral days, strong solar insolation will promote deep boundary \nlayer mixing by mid-afternoon, which may promote isolated light to \nmoderate rain showers favoring the eastern half of our area where \nmoisture quality is slightly better. With no upper level support and \nmeager instability, this activity will pose little more threat than \na few lightning strikes. Tonight, light winds and mostly clear skies \nwill foster Sunday morning lows in the mid 60s to mid 70s.\n\nRinse and repeat the forecast for Sunday, as no change in the \nsynoptic pattern results in another ~1 degree C of warming at 850-mb \nand afternoon highs at the surface increasing by another degree or \ntwo. Likewise, strong insolation will engender the same isolated \nlight to moderate rain showers that pose only a severely limited \nlightning threat.\n\nMonday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nthe upper ridge will shift slightly southeastward such that \nsouthwest KS will be nearly or directly under its core. This subtle \npattern change will not have profound implications for the sensible \nweather across the central plains, however it will promote stronger \nsubsidence aloft which will manifest in afternoon temperatures \nwarming closer to normal with highs in the mid to upper 90s. \nAdditionally, as the core of the ridge reaches near the central and \nsouthern plains interface, westerly flow across the central Rockies \nwill re-establish, inducing lee troughing to return and allowing \nsoutherly winds over our area to increase. As for precipitation \nchances, the bulk of southwest KS will remain dry through the end of \nthe week, although weak disturbances diving southeast on the eastern \nperiphery of the upper ridge may support precipitation clipping our \nnorthern zones by mid-week. That said, confidence is very low.",
